<!-- Website teaching layer for android/kotlin-multiplatform/ui-sharing/resource-sharing.md. Original preserved in full. Structure defined in android-phase9-structure.md before drafting. Checked 2026-10-25 against https://kotlinlang.org/docs/multiplatform/compose-multiplatform-resources-setup.html. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A translated string works in a developer build but is missing from the published consumer app. The accessor compiled, while the resource’s journey through generation and packaging was never checked. Both are part of the delivered contract.</p>

Follow one approved resource from its source to its actual reader, keeping native and shared UI ownership explicit.

<!-- @after Current Baseline :: visual -->
### One source can produce different platform outputs

<figure class="viz"><table><caption>Follow the shipped translation, not only its accessor</caption><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1</th><td>Reviewed string: placeholders and fallback</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2</th><td>Generated API: names and types</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">3</th><td>Published payload: actual packaged resources</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">4</th><td>Final app: locale selection and rendered text</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>The boundaries in this example are different responsibilities, not implied packet transfers.</figcaption></figure>

Use one checkout confirmation string to separate the failure boundaries. The reviewed input includes its placeholders and fallback. Resource generation supplies the API the caller compiles against; packaging must also carry the actual resource payload into the distribution consumed by the app. A successful accessor call in a developer build does not establish that a separately published library contains everything the release consumer needs.

Build a clean consumer from the exact published version and load the string in the relevant locale. Check missing translations, placeholder mismatches and enlarged text at the rendered surface. If native UI needs a generated native catalog, make that a deliberate output with an owner rather than assuming a Compose accessor becomes a platform resource automatically. Retain the source identity and final consumer result so a missing production string can be traced to input, generation, publication or application integration.

<!-- @after 6. Packaging, Publication, and Raw Files :: animation -->
### A string travels to the consumer

<figure class="stepper" data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A string travels to the consumer</span><div class="stage viz-flow"><div class="node" data-el="n0"><strong>Approved catalog</strong><small data-text="v0">—</small></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="node" data-el="n1"><strong>Generation</strong><small data-text="v1">—</small></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="node" data-el="n2"><strong>Published artifact</strong><small data-text="v2">—</small></div><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span><div class="node" data-el="n3"><strong>Consumer app</strong><small data-text="v3">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Approve", "caption": "A reviewed translation includes its required placeholders and default value. Structural checks run before it becomes a build input.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "active", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Versioned input", "v1": "Not generated", "v2": "Not packaged", "v3": "Old version"}}, {"label": "Generate", "caption": "The configured resource task generates supported accessors and target resources. Product callers use the declared API.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "active", "n2": "idle", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Stable ownership", "v1": "Accessor + resource", "v2": "Build input ready", "v3": "Consumer still unverified"}}, {"label": "Package", "caption": "Publish the library through the supported integration path. Retain the intended public resource API and required payload.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "active", "n3": "idle"}, "text": {"v0": "Traceable input", "v1": "Generated output", "v2": "Exact version", "v3": "Consumes that version"}}, {"label": "Verify", "caption": "Load the string in the final app under the required locale and text size. A library compile alone cannot establish correct packaging or layout.", "add": ["n0", "n1", "n2", "n3"], "state": {"n0": "idle", "n1": "idle", "n2": "idle", "n3": "active"}, "text": {"v0": "Source trace retained", "v1": "Contract checked", "v2": "Distribution tested", "v3": "Rendered and tested"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence, not a measured trace. <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/multiplatform/compose-multiplatform-resources-setup.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Primary contract</a>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Common Failure Modes :: check -->
### Try the decision yourself

<details><summary>Does a public Res class prove a font was packaged?</summary><p>No. Verify runtime lookup in the final consumer artifact.</p></details>
<details><summary>Should the domain return translated copy?</summary><p>Keep domain outcomes separate from presentation-localization ownership where that boundary fits the product.</p></details>
<details><summary>Can one translation source serve native and shared UI?</summary><p>Yes, with deliberately generated target representations and validation; native catalogs are not automatically supplied by Kotlin accessors.</p></details>

